   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.2",
    "topic_title": "Environmental Impacts on Enzyme Function",
    "stem": "A student measures the activity of an enzyme from human cells (micromoles of product formed per minute) at several temperatures: 20°C, 12; 30°C, 25; 37°C, 40; 45°C, 22; 55°C, 3. A separate sample of the enzyme is held at 55°C for 10 minutes, cooled to 37°C, and tested again; its activity is 4. Which explanation best accounts for both the drop in activity above 37°C and the result after cooling?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Temperatures above 37°C reduce how often enzyme and substrate molecules collide, so fewer enzyme-substrate complexes form and the rate falls.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the collision idea. Raising temperature increases molecular motion and collision frequency; the rate falls above the optimum because the enzyme's shape changes, not because collisions decrease. It also cannot explain why activity stayed low after cooling. Fix: Ask whether a higher temperature speeds or slows molecular motion before using collisions to explain a rate decrease."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Heat above 37°C broke the peptide bonds between amino acids, destroying the enzyme's primary structure so it could no longer catalyze the reaction.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses denaturation with breaking the amino acid chain. Heat denaturation disrupts weaker interactions such as hydrogen bonds that maintain higher-level folding; the covalent peptide bonds of the primary structure typically remain intact. Fix: Check which level of protein structure depends on weak interactions before naming what heat disrupts."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme was only slowed at 55°C and returned to its normal shape and full activity once it was cooled back to 37°C.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes denaturation is always reversible and ignores the data. The cooled sample's activity was 4, far below the 40 measured at 37°C, so the enzyme did not regain its functional shape. Fix: Compare the post-cooling activity value to the original 37°C value before claiming the enzyme recovered."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Heat above the optimum disrupted hydrogen bonds and other interactions that hold the enzyme's three-dimensional shape, so the active site no longer fit the substrate, and this denaturation was not reversed by cooling.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "This links the data to structure. Above 37°C, increased molecular motion breaks the weak interactions that maintain tertiary structure, altering the active site so substrate binds poorly. Activity after cooling stayed near 4 rather than returning to 40, showing the shape change persisted, so the denaturation was not reversed."
     }
    ]
   },
